    "created_at": "2016-10-19T23:17:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-19T23:18:05Z",
    "body": "> but the docs do describe auto-detection of dfn types\n\nAnd note that those docs do _not_ say that `<dfn>'foo'</dfn>` will get detected as a value - I detect _double single quotes_ as values, so you can do `<dfn>''foo''</dfn>` (because `''foo''` is usually a value-link).\n\n(That said, the auto-detection only looks for CSS types, and can result in unexpected typing; I should really put them behind a pref and turn it on in the CSS/FX/Houdini defaults.)\n\nNobody publishing a Bikeshedded document experiences this problem, because Bikeshed tags _every_ dfn with a `data-dfn-type` in its output, so Shepherd's additional auto-detection doesn't ever fire. It's just the non-Bikeshed docs getting spidered that get hit with this.  And, I reiterate, this is using _different_ heuristics than Bikeshed does anyway; this has nothing to do with Bikeshed and is a Shepherd issue. :/\n\nBut also: the definitions in question are only causing problems because _they shouldn't be definitions in the Bikeshed database anyway_. Trying to figure out how to stop them from getting auto-detected (so they'll default to \"dfn\" type and not be exported) is just sweeping the problem under the rug; Bikeshed is still going to see bad definitions.  I already implemented a way to handle this (dfn elements intended for use in a different processor, which shouldn't be exposed to Bikeshed) - just put `data-lt=\"\"` on them.  That's the correct solution to this whole thing.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
